## Key Design Decisions

| Decision | Rationale |
|---|---|
| **Index tagging before sort** | Guarantees stable sort regardless of JS engine implementation |
| **getCharPriority helper** | Clean separation of the "numbers before letters" rule |
| **Character-by-character ASCII loop** | Precise descending ASCII comparison beyond just the first character |
| **Defensive input validation** | Production-ready robustness |
| **Pure function (returns new array)** | No mutation of the original array — follows functional programming best practices |

## Solution: Generating Evenly Distributed Random Numbers in C++ (0-19)

While this is fundamentally a technical problem rather than a business problem, I'll approach it with the same analytical rigor — breaking it down clearly and giving you the **most robust, production-quality solution**.

---

### Root Cause Analysis of Common Pitfalls

Before jumping to code, let's identify what **most developers get wrong**:

| Mistake | Problem |
|---|---|
| Using `rand() % 20` | Biased distribution (modulo bias) |
| Using `rand()` without seeding | Same sequence every run |
| Using `time()` as seed | Poor entropy, predictable |
| Using old C-style random | Not thread-safe, low quality |

---

### Recommended Solution: Modern C++11 Approach

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <random>

int generateRandomNumber() {
    // Step 1: High-quality, non-deterministic seed
    std::random_device rd;

    // Step 2: Mersenne Twister engine - industry standard PRNG
    std::mt19937 engine(rd());

    // Step 3: TRULY uniform distribution - no modulo bias
    std::uniform_int_distribution<int> distribution(0, 19);

    return distribution(engine);
}

int main() {
    // Generate and display 20 random numbers
    std::cout << "Generated random numbers (0-19):\n";
    for (int i = 0; i < 20; ++i) {
        std::cout << generateRandomNumber() << " ";
    }
    std::cout << std::endl;

    return 0;
}
```

---

### Performance-Optimized Version (Recommended for Production)

If you're generating **large volumes** of numbers, avoid re-initializing the engine on every call:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <random>

class RandomNumberGenerator {
private:
    std::mt19937 engine;
    std::uniform_int_distribution<int> distribution;

public:
    // Constructor initializes once - highly efficient
    RandomNumberGenerator()
        : engine(std::random_device{}())
        , distribution(0, 19) {}

    int generate() {
        return distribution(engine);
    }

    // Bonus: Verify distribution quality
    void runDistributionTest(int sampleSize) {
        int frequency[20] = {0};

        for (int i = 0; i < sampleSize; ++i) {
            frequency[generate()]++;
        }

        std::cout << "\nDistribution Test (" << sampleSize << " samples):\n";
        std::cout << "--------------------------------\n";
        double expectedPct = 100.0 / 20;

        for (int i = 0; i < 20; ++i) {
            double actualPct = (frequency[i] * 100.0) / sampleSize;
            std::cout << "Number " << i
                      << ": " << frequency[i]
                      << " times ("
                      << actualPct << "% vs expected "
                      << expectedPct << "%)\n";
        }
    }
};

int main() {
    RandomNumberGenerator rng;

    // Single number generation
    std::cout << "Single random number: " << rng.generate() << "\n";

    // Verify even distribution with 100,000 samples
    rng.runDistributionTest(100000);

    return 0;
}
```

---

### Expected Output (Distribution Test)

```
Distribution Test (100000 samples):
--------------------------------
Number 0:  4987 times (4.987% vs expected 5%)
Number 1:  5023 times (5.023% vs expected 5%)
Number 2:  4998 times (4.998% vs expected 5%)
... (all values converging tightly around 5%)
```

---

### Key Decision Rationale

| Component | Choice | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `std::random_device` | Seed source | True hardware entropy |
| `std::mt19937` | PRNG Engine | 19,937-bit state, statistically proven uniform |
| `uniform_int_distribution` | Distribution | Mathematically eliminates modulo bias |
| Class encapsulation | Architecture | Engine initialized once = optimal performance |

---

### Bottom Line

> **Use `std::mt19937` + `std::uniform_int_distribution` — always.** This is the C++ standard for a reason. It gives you cryptographically seeded, statistically verified, bias-free random numbers with minimal code complexity.

Compile with: `g++ -std=c++11 -o random_gen random_gen.cpp`

